```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}

\begin{document}

We wish to maintain a data structure containing sets of numbers. Let's assume each set has a unique identifier number. We define a continuous interval of set identifiers as follows: Given a sorted array \(A\) of all set identifiers, a continuous interval of identifiers is a set containing all elements in a subarray of \(A\).

For example: Suppose the data structure contains sets with the identifiers \(\{1, 13, 42, 50\}\) at a given moment. Then:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The set \(\{1, 13\}\) is a continuous interval.
  \item The set \(\{13, 42, 250\}\) is a continuous interval.
  \item The set \(\{1, 42\}\) is not a continuous interval.
\end{itemize}

Let \(n\) denote the number of sets, and \(m\) denote the maximum size of a set. When possible, express the complexity in terms of \(m\), \(n\), and \(|B|\).

Implement a random data structure that supports the following operations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{createSet(setId)}: Create a set with the identifier \texttt{setId}.
  \item \texttt{insertToSets(B, E)}: Given an array containing elements of a continuous interval of identifiers \(B\) (existing in the structure) and an array of elements \(E\), insert the element \(E[i]\) into the set with the identifier \(B[i]\). Both arrays have the same length.
\end{itemize}

\end{document}
```

